WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:11.379
The design of this Little Trees car freshener goes way back to 1952 in Watertown, New York.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:14.500
And an unhappy milkman who was complaining to one of his customers

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.940
about the bad smell of spoiled milk.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.120
Lucky for him, he was talking to Julius Samann, a Jewish chemist who’d fled from Nazi Germany

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:27.560
and spent time living in the forests of Canada
studying the aroma of alpine trees.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.360
After his chance encounter with the milkman, Samann started to create

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.660
paper soaked in natural pine oil.

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:39.930
A string was also attached to stop people
getting the perfume all over their hands.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:45.590
The freshener was originally in the shape
of a buxom pin up girl, but Samann changed

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:49.190
his design to the pine tree we know today.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:53.800
This allowed the cellophane wrapper to be
rolled down slowly, so the fragrance would

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.390
last that little bit longer.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.800
As the automotive industry took off so
did Little Trees.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.820
Filling the air with classics like Black Ice,
Royal Pine, Vanillaroma

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.320
and of course New Car Scent.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.100
Fragrant and affordable, this is the design
that brings you

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.720
the heady aroma of a Canadian woodland.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.470
Whether you’re in a milk cart or a taxi
cab.

